db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f507c9e1-30ff-5ca4-a4e4-d03f72191b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(70,0.52),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X\\ge 37).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.5040\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01c1e9f3-92c7-5436-aa3f-7f0248f68a95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495048 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=70$ and $p=0.52.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70\\cdot 0.52 \\\\  & =36.4 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70\\cdot (1-0.52) \\\\  & =33.6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(70)(0.52)=36.4\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(70)(0.52)(1-0.52)=17.472.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(36.4,17.472).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 37).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈P(Y\\ge 36.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 36.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36.5-36.4}{\\sqrt{17.472}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.02) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.02) \\\\  & =1-0.5080 \\\\  & =0.4920\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈0.492.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.4905.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1071 "On average, $53%$ of the emails Emily receives are spam. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that she has $70$ or more spam emails in her inbox if she has $140$ emails in her inbox. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "036e3017-f74f-5cab-a417-486991609067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495051 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=140$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =140\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =74.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =140\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =65.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(140)(0.53)=74.2\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(140)(0.53)(1-0.53)=34.874.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(74.2,34.874).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈P(Y\\ge 69.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 69.5) & =1-P(Y<69.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<\\frac{69.5-74.2}{\\sqrt{34.874}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =1-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈0.788.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.7869.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of finding more than $120$ people with blood type O+ in a randomly selected sample of $200$ if $53%$ of the population has blood type O+.\n\nII. The probability of finding less than $4$ defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine if $2%$ of the items made are defective.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on heads $9$ times out of $15$ coin flips if the probability of a single flip landing on heads is $0.25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "149f319f-bbde-53de-865d-1514ecb7ea83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495062 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =106 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =94 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.02.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=15$ and $p=0.25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =3.75 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495068 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495068 #edb/ref 1071 "In a math test, a student has a $55%$ probability of correctly answering each question. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that a student correctly solves more than $10$ questions of a $20$-question test. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | 