.44}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{67.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.90\\le Z\\le -0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z<-0.90) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z\\le -0.90) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.74)-Φ(-0.90) \\\\  & =0.2297-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.046\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈0.046.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0468.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair spinner with five regions, red, green, yellow, orange, and blue, lands on yellow in $8$ out of $15$ spins.\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player misses a penalty if the probability of scoring the penalty is $0.48.$\n\nIII. The probability that an archer hits the bull's eye more than $10$ times out of $25$ attempts if the probability of hitting the bull's eye on a single shot is $0.56.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "836c76f0-231f-583b-b08e-df25ef466c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495105 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=1$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =1\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.48 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(240,0.45),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X>100).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "865aae44-8837-5a5c-9a79-489c7c05763a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495106 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=240$ and $p=0.45.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =240\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =108 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =240\\cdot (1-0.45) \\\\  & =132 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(240)(0.45)=108\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(240)(0.45)(1-0.45)=59.4.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(108,59.4).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>100).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>100)=P(X\\ge 101)≈P(Y\\ge 100.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 100.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{100.5-108}{\\sqrt{59.4}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge -0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.97) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.97) \\\\  & =1-0.1660 \\\\  & =0.834\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>100)≈0.834.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.8347.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(85,0.56),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=50).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b726722f-b10f-58df-8eed-f61e3c1a5880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495132 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=85$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =85\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =47.6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =85\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =37.4 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(85)(0.56)=47.6\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(85)(0.56)(1-0.56)=20.94.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(47.6,20.94).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.42\\le Z\\le 0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z<0.42) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z\\le 0.42) \\\\  & =Φ(0.63)-Φ(0.42) \\\\  & =0.7357-0.6628 \\\\  & =0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.073.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0758.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495137 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a soccer player scores $16$ out of $25$ penalties if the player has a $58%$ probability of scoring each penalty. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\