b/ref 1069 "q-95743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495164 #edb/ref 1071 "In an airport, $58%$ of the flights arrive early. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $60$ flights are picked at random, then $40$ of them will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed942805-cf1d-5ddc-afc6-5eb5833ba5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495164 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=60$ and $p=0.58.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.58 \\\\  & =34.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.58) \\\\  & =25.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(60)(0.58)=34.8\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(60)(0.58)(1-0.58)=14.62.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(34.8,14.62).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=40).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5) & =P(\\frac{39.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{40.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.23\\le Z\\le 1.49) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z<1.23) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z\\le 1.23) \\\\  & =Φ(1.49)-Φ(1.23) \\\\  & =0.9319-0.8907 \\\\  & =0.0412\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈0.041.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0415.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1071 "In a biased coin, $60%$ of the flips land on heads. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the coin lands on heads at least $21$ times out of $50$ flips. Give your final answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0017\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0022\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0032\\,$ | $\\,0.0031\\,$ | $\\,0.0030\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fff943eb-16aa-5bd0-bfca-c95efa66ad92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495174 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(50)(0.6)=30\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(50)(0.6)(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(30,12).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 21).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈P(Y>20.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>20.5) & =1-P(Y\\le 20.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{20.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -2.74) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.74) \\\\  & =1-0.0031 \\\\  & =0.9969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈0.997.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.9966.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
